#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096562 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-745" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=e^{x}$ and let $P_2(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_2(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{1.1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(3)}(x)|=e^x$ is an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(3)}(1.1)| =e^{1.1},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}|0.1|^{3}   \\approx 0.000\\,501,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-746" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\ln(1-x)$ and let $P_3(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_3(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln(0.9)$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_3$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial $P_3$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{4!}|x-0|^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.1.$ Plugging $x=0.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|0.1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(4)}(x)|=\\dfrac{6}{(x-1)^4}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0,0.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(4)}(0.1)| =\\dfrac{6}{(0.1-1)^4} = \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{ \\left( \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4} \\right) }{24}|0.1|^{4}  \\approx 0.000\\,038,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198af1c0-5c8f-56ac-b8d0-73fd97e93b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529629} {:answer/id #uuid "221295e3-b7a7-5e11-a8d8-39994098832c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.017" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529652} {:answer/id #uuid "380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.046" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529707} {:answer/id #uuid "82ebe979-ce57-57ec-99a9-a11aee8474c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.009" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529904} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af7625-c031-5f9a-8448-5e0736d3e728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.046" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529707} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f20a2fb-0d1a-5ecb-8bf0-40ff423fe5fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5e88433-85a5-5481-9536-7dfae4f91322" :question/math-academy-id "q-103852" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=(1+x)^{3/2}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $(1.9)^{3/2}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.9.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.9$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.9-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.9{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.9].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1+x)^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1+x)^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2}|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.9],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+0)^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{6}|0.9{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)^{3}}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.046.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529756} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee810e-caf1-5778-8d50-ddcea2fbc768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529812} {:answer/id #uuid "60fb0da3-26a9-54ba-8f00-0b915d7c609f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529816} {:answer/id #uuid "d11dc14d-c3d7-598f-81c0-4c080c11ce7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530104} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d65d11-05f8-529f-a9a0-2faa772f8a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529756} :answer-field/id #uuid "505704bc-04a4-5876-b503-4f2abf719102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "948d9306-7c7c-59c6-bfff-2f8bea699654" :question/math-academy-id "q-103856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{-3.3}={e}^{-3(1.1)}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1.1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-27{e}^{-3x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-27{e}^{-3x}|=27{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=27{e}^{-3x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=27{e}^{-3(1)}=\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}})}{6}|0.1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2{e}^{3}}\\cdot 0.001 \\\\  & ≈0.0002.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbe50e9-7976-59a7-89c3-05577c4d4e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529673} {:answer/id #uuid "40a27b19-7d0a-5731-a5f5-80e7d90ea7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529732} {:answer/id #uuid "79fa3435-6ee8-5d6f-8f2d-f21bcdc60b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529890} {:answer/id #uuid "99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529957} {:answer/id #uuid "adaee6c3-bba1-5f68-9ad5-e21e92e1228b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529957} :answer-field/id #uuid "4166e2b7-c9ea-52ad-b2a3-8d0fb32213b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103872" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|2-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{8}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,2],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{8(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{8})}{6}|1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nup to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529582} {:answer/id #uuid "11c234cf-17eb-5252-9439-261d6cb33e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529612} {:answer/id #uuid "17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529626} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6c9f8d-9261-5310-bdc5-6b757360cea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529675} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8d020f-a077-5090-baab-35bc37c195f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529626} :answer-field/id #uuid "287ab9b4-ef99-51aa-b119-51a1269f75d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103882" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|2{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}|=\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,3],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{16(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{16},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{16})}{24}|2{|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {2}^{4}}{16\\cdot 24} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529634} {:answer/id #uuid "9688ddeb-1dfd-5f91-9a17-85eb71116616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529945} {:answer/id #uuid "9dca8394-0fd7-5eab-89fc-e1babcf985e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529971} {:answer/id #uuid "b67fece6-0ac9-535e-8151-8eeb845f93a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530037} {:answer/id #uuid "eea68c41-0fda-5c95-b448-41701dba855a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529634} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedf09a2-2732-57b4-bde6-4d08d850c1fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc2772cd-2bc4-5ed7-b988-82cf9e52af83" :question/math-academy-id "q-103900" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "540451fd-fe97-5678-bf5f-389fd52f9b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.003\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529781} {:answer/id #uuid "a73028cb-a36c-5800-a191-1ab9cada333b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.002\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529995} {:answer/id #uuid "b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.001\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530034} {:answer/id #uuid "c8762886-22b3-5e2b-9614-762f57eb3d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.004\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530085} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ff2cb8-a71a-56c9-ba60-836e6a71e4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.001\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530034} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd32982-99ac-5951-be9c-a7d6abcdb3a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f0c0766-f92b-5029-93d1-2aeb7633b5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-11585" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{0.65}.$ Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=0.65$ and $a=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.65$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.65-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0.65,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0.65,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}≈0.001\\,7,\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529711} {:answer/id #uuid "45c01446-829f-5f53-b773-5a46add2a345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529743} {:answer/id #uuid "a9779fc8-c256-59bd-b94b-25cb9d4c9cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530001} {:answer/id #uuid "add5f89b-e7d4-5720-9518-dfe4679a52c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530012} {:answer/id #uuid "f610a192-d5e1-5235-b2a1-ce3249d5f8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529711} :answer-field/id #uuid "969e58d0-648c-5c59-91c2-cc3ac39b94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6eeece9-3543-5575-94c0-d71f28347d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-13972" :question/problem "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of the number $\\frac{1}{e}.$ Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{-0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{9}{8\\,000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529614} {:answer/id #uuid "3c49d6b0-1cee-5345-9267-3413b034af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{9}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529722} {:answer/id #uuid "548b7b40-9324-5d50-a272-34d07facd245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{3}{11\\,000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529782} {:answer/id #uuid "58e49fdf-507c-5cd4-9d51-1bf8b6e8b879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{3}{800}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529794} {:answer/id #uuid "fe19f1ce-1baf-5ea3-a712-8dedf9961f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{27}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{9}{8\\,000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529614} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fc0622-6b89-5e60-84fd-c344c741bb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bfbcc6e-0a0e-51f6-8574-b8c524edf820" :question/math-academy-id "q-51342" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.3.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-2{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=2$ and $x=2.3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2.3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}\\,{|2.3-2|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[2,2.3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[2,2.3],$ its value is greatest at $x=2.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(2)|=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{0.3}^{3}}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f33b43-4368-59b6-9c7d-d1d7b630d1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529618} {:answer/id #uuid "3d98ce0f-129f-51bf-9a3e-c9f63afae9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529723} {:answer/id #uuid "556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529784} {:answer/id #uuid "64e0788d-8a4e-5cb8-aa06-7cbe63186d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529832} {:answer/id #uuid "ddec7804-92b7-519d-b753-624b291a0137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529784} :answer-field/id #uuid "26eade32-ac9c-595f-b7c2-c1d810c396a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c81fea43-7a0e-5b24-b7d9-362bddbf7de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81409" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}={e}^{-0.5}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.5.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.5$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.5-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.5{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.5].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.5],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{1}{6}|0.5{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot {2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61926743-8db2-5522-a575-4884614fd77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529819} {:answer/id #uuid "67dee9ee-b688-5a6c-85b3-9f9d1783148a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529840} {:answer/id #uuid "a1474263-af15-566e-8a13-b2427579c1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529979} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530024} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa8715-79f1-5f77-accf-e8e0b0e15fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530024} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec1a6f23-dffc-5c92-b6f1-97a12771feda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e46215f5-878f-5a8e-bbfe-fcdc781327b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81411" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{e}}={e}^{-1/3}.$ Round your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $a=0.$ \n\nPlugging $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}{|-\\frac{1}{3}-0|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{1}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & ≈0.000\\,51,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places."}]}
